-- Q12083: Intangible Assets [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["As the excess of acquisition cost over identifiable net asset fair value in a business combination","When internally generated goodwill becomes reliably measurable by the reporting entity","When market capitalization exceeds the book value of the entity''s total equity","When management estimates the value of the entity''s reputation and expected synergies"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 805-30-25-1, goodwill is recognized only in a business combination as the excess of consideration transferred over the fair value of identifiable net assets acquired. Pinnacle''s $5 million excess ($22M − $17M) is recorded as goodwill.
Wrong (B): U.S. GAAP prohibits recognition of internally generated goodwill regardless of measurement reliability.
Wrong (C): A market capitalization exceeding book value may suggest unrecognized value but does not trigger goodwill recognition under GAAP.
Wrong (D): Management estimates of reputation and synergies do not qualify for recognition without an acquisition transaction.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12083;

-- Q12091: Inventory [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["As a selling expense reported on the income statement","As an inventoriable cost included in the raw materials balance","As a general and administrative expense for the period","As a separate asset classified under prepaid freight"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 330-10-30, all costs necessary to bring inventory to its present location and condition are inventoriable. Freight-in of $3,500 is capitalized alongside the $60,000 materials cost.
Wrong (A): Freight-in is not a selling expense; freight-out (delivery to customers) is classified as selling expense.
Wrong (C): Transportation costs to acquire inventory are product costs, not period costs charged to G&A.
Wrong (D): Freight-in is not recorded as a separate prepaid asset; it becomes part of inventory cost.$EXPL$
