-- Q12174: Payables and Accrued Liabilities [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Recognized as miscellaneous income on the income statement","Reversed against retained earnings as a prior-period adjustment","Carried as a liability on the balance sheet indefinitely","Remitted to the applicable state government under escheat laws"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 405-20 and state escheat laws, entities holding dormant funds such as outstanding checks or customer credit balances must remit these amounts to the state after the statutory dormancy period. The liability transfers to the state.
Wrong (A): Recognizing unclaimed property as income is generally improper; the obligation transfers to the state, not to earnings.
Wrong (B): Escheatment is not a prior-period adjustment; it is a current-period transfer of an existing liability.
Wrong (C): Entities cannot carry these amounts indefinitely; state statutes require remittance after the dormancy period.$EXPL$
