-- Q12183: Revenue Recognition [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$200,000 because the slotting fee is classified as a separate marketing expense","$215,000 because the slotting fee increases the total consideration exchanged between parties","$200,000 with the slotting fee disclosed separately in the financial statement notes","$185,000 because the slotting fee reduces the transaction price for non-distinct consideration"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 606-10-32-25, consideration payable to a customer is accounted for as a reduction of the transaction price unless it is in exchange for a distinct good or service. Since the shelf space is not distinct, the $15,000 reduces the price to $185,000.
Wrong (A): ASC 606 does not permit separate expense treatment when consideration paid to a customer is not for a distinct good or service.
Wrong (B): The slotting fee is paid by the seller to the customer; it reduces rather than increases the transaction price.
Wrong (C): Disclosure alone is insufficient; the fee must be reflected as a reduction in the transaction price.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12183;

-- Q12184: Revenue Recognition [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Substantive reason, product identified as the customer''s, ready for transfer, and entity cannot redirect it","Full customer payment received and a signed nonrefundable purchase agreement in place","Insurance obtained on the product with the customer named as the designated beneficiary","Product segregated in a bonded warehouse with the customer holding the warehouse access key"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 606-10-25-30, bill-and-hold revenue recognition requires all four criteria: (1) substantive reason for the arrangement, (2) product separately identified as the customer''s, (3) product currently ready for physical transfer, and (4) entity cannot use or direct the product elsewhere.
Wrong (B): Payment alone does not satisfy bill-and-hold criteria; all four conditions must be met regardless of payment status.
Wrong (C): Obtaining insurance is not one of the four required criteria, though it may be a practical consideration.
Wrong (D): Physical segregation alone does not establish that all four ASC 606 bill-and-hold conditions are satisfied.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12184;

-- Q12185: Revenue Recognition [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Disclose the coupon as a contingent obligation and recognize the full $800 as revenue immediately","Allocate a portion of the $800 transaction price to the coupon as a separate performance obligation","Recognize the coupon discount as a marketing expense at the time of customer redemption","Defer the entire $800 of revenue until the coupon either expires or is redeemed by the customer"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 606-10-55-41 through 55-45, a customer option providing a material right (discount not available to other customers) is a separate performance obligation. The $800 transaction price is allocated between the smartphone and the material right using relative standalone selling prices.
Wrong (A): A material right requires allocation of the transaction price, not mere disclosure as a contingent obligation.
Wrong (C): The coupon provides an incremental benefit not available to all customers, making it a performance obligation rather than a marketing cost.
Wrong (D): Deferring the entire $800 is excessive; only the portion allocated to the material right is deferred.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12185;

-- Q12188: Revenue Recognition [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["As a lease because the repurchase price is below the original selling price and return is incentivized","As a sale with a separate put option valued at $20,000 on the transaction date","As a sale with full $400,000 revenue because the call option is at the entity''s discretion","As a financing arrangement with the $400,000 recorded as a deposit liability"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 606-10-55-70 through 55-72, when an entity has a call option to repurchase an asset at a price lower than the original selling price, the arrangement is accounted for as a lease if the customer has a significant economic incentive to return the asset. Since $380,000 exceeds the expected market value of $350,000, the customer benefits from returning it.
Wrong (B): The contract contains a call option held by the seller, not a put option; the customer does not have the right to require repurchase.
Wrong (C): ASC 606 requires evaluating the economic substance of repurchase options regardless of which party holds the option.
Wrong (D): Financing treatment applies when the repurchase price equals or exceeds the original selling price, not when it is lower.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12188;

-- Q12195: Revenue Recognition [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Three separate performance obligations since each deliverable has standalone value","Two performance obligations: the integration service separate from the combined deliverables","Three separate obligations with the integration service recognized on a ratable basis","One combined performance obligation because significant integration eliminates separability"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 606-10-25-21(b), even when goods or services are individually capable of providing benefit, they are not distinct if the entity provides a significant integration service that substantially modifies the components. All three items form a single combined performance obligation.
Wrong (A): The ''capable of being distinct'' criterion alone is insufficient; goods must also be ''separately identifiable'' within the contract context.
Wrong (B): The integration service cannot be separated from the items it integrates when it substantially modifies each component.
Wrong (C): Ratable recognition of the integration service implies separability, which contradicts the significant integration finding.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12195;

-- Q12199: Revenue Recognition [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["When end customers purchase sofas because the consignment means control has not transferred","When Fieldstone delivers the sofas to HomeStyle because physical possession has transferred","Ratably over the expected selling period of the consigned inventory at HomeStyle","When HomeStyle accepts the sofas and places them on the showroom display floor"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 606-10-55-80, indicators of a consignment arrangement include the ability to return the product and no obligation to pay until goods are sold. Control remains with Fieldstone until the end customer purchases, so revenue is recognized at that point.
Wrong (B): Physical delivery alone does not transfer control when consignment indicators such as return rights and contingent payment are present.
Wrong (C): Ratable recognition has no basis under ASC 606 for consignment arrangements; revenue follows the transfer of control.
Wrong (D): Acceptance and display by the consignee does not constitute transfer of control when the consignee can return unsold goods.$EXPL$
