-- Q12320: Statement of Cash Flows [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Operating flows translated at the ending rate with the exchange line capturing the average-rate difference","Operating flows translated at the average rate with the exchange line reconciling spot-rate differences on balances","Operating flows translated at the beginning rate with the exchange line capturing total currency movement","All subsidiary flows translated at the ending rate so no exchange rate effect line is necessary"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 830-230-45-1, a foreign subsidiary''s cash flows are translated at rates in effect at the time of the flows, approximated by the weighted-average rate. The exchange rate effect line reconciles differences arising because beginning cash uses the beginning spot rate, ending cash uses the ending spot rate, and flows use the average rate.
Wrong (A): The ending rate is used for the ending cash balance, not for translating period operating flows.
Wrong (C): The beginning rate applies to the opening cash balance, not to operating cash flows during the period.
Wrong (D): Using only the ending rate would eliminate the reconciling difference, which contradicts ASC 830 requirements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12320;

-- Q12323: Statement of Cash Flows [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Subtract $85,000 because stock compensation is an equity transaction not an operating charge","Add $85,000 back to net income as a noncash charge similar to depreciation expense","No adjustment is needed because stock compensation is already excluded from net income","Report $85,000 as a financing outflow because it relates to equity-based instruments"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 230-10-45-28, stock-based compensation expense is a noncash charge added back to net income under the indirect method, identical in treatment to depreciation. Both reduce net income without consuming cash, so both are added back in operating activities.
Wrong (A): Noncash expenses that reduced net income are always added back, not subtracted, in the indirect method reconciliation.
Wrong (C): Stock compensation expense is included in net income as a charge to compensation expense; an add-back adjustment is required.
Wrong (D): The expense is a noncash operating item; it does not represent a financing cash outflow.$EXPL$
